{# An account's folder rows, without the frame around them.

   Two callers: the frame response below (`_account_labels.html.twig`), for an
   account the user just expanded, and the sidebar itself, which renders the
   already-expanded account's rows straight into the page — a list fetched one
   request after the sidebar is a list that blinks on every navigation.

   Each entry carries `account`, which narrows the label view to this account.
   Without it these linked to the plain label view, and since labels are
   user-scoped and bind to several accounts at once, opening "Receipts" under
   one account listed every account's Receipts — read as this account's, and it
   was not. The sidebar's own label section still links without it, because
   there the label genuinely does mean "across everything". #}

{# No pl-3 on the nav: the indent is depth now, so the pill still reaches the
   sidebar's left edge like every other row rather than starting three units
   in. #}

{# An entrance only on the fetched path, and this is why the flag exists rather
   than a plain `data-enter` on the nav. These rows have two lives: rendered
   into the sidebar for the already-expanded account, where they are simply part
   of the page and animating them would mean the sidebar blinking on every
   navigation; and fetched into a frame when somebody expands a different
   account, where they genuinely do arrive out of nowhere a request later. Only
   the second one is an arrival, and only the second one is told so. #}
<nav class="nav-rows"{% if entering|default(false) %} data-enter="fade"{% endif %}>
    {% for label in labels %}
        {# The same .nav-item pill as the rest of the sidebar, and a sidebar
           link target so the open folder is highlighted at all — these rows had
           a rounded-lg hover box and no active state whatsoever. Depth rides in
           --label-depth, which the pill adds to, rather than an inline
           padding-left the pill would know nothing about. #}
        {# The row is the pill and the anchor is what fills it, rather than the
           anchor being both — the badge beside it is a link of its own now, and
           an <a> inside an <a> is not markup that survives parsing. Same shape
           the system rows and the label tree use. #}
        {# Somewhere a conversation from THIS account can be dropped.

           Account-scoped, unlike the shared label rows above: a label spans
           every account, but the folder behind it does not — the binding that
           links a label to a real IMAP mailbox is per account. Dropping a
           conversation from one mailbox onto another's Receipts would attach a
           label with no folder on that side, and the mail would be filed by a
           fallback rather than where it was let go. mail--dnd refuses it.

           Sent, Drafts and Snoozed take no drops. See
           LabelRole::acceptsMoves(), which is also what the server checks. #}
        {% import '_partials/_sidebar.html.twig' as sidebarMacros %}
        {% set droppable = label.role is null or label.role.acceptsMoves %}
        <div class="nav-item flex items-center justify-between text-xs transition-colors text-ink-muted"
             {% if droppable %}{{ sidebarMacros.drop_target(label.id, account) }}{% endif %}
             data-nav-tier="tree"
             style="--label-depth: {{ 12 + label.depth * 12 }}px">
            <a
                data-ui--sidebar-target="link"
                href="{{ path('app_mail_label', { id: label.id, account: account.id }) }}"
                data-turbo-frame="_top"
                data-turbo-prefetch="false"
                class="flex-1 min-w-0 truncate"
            >{{ label|label_name }}</a>
            {% if not label.isSystem %}
                {# Counted within this account, and keyed that way too: the
                   sidebar's own label rows carry `label:<id>` and mean every
                   account, so sharing the key would have the counts endpoint
                   patch the account's badge with the total.

                   The unread view it opens carries the same `account=`, so the
                   list matches the number that was clicked — the whole reason
                   this count is account-scoped in the first place. #}
                {% import '_partials/_sidebar.html.twig' as sidebar %}
                {{ sidebar.unread_badge(
                    sidebarCounts.forLabelInAccount(label, account),
                    'label:' ~ label.id ~ ':account:' ~ account.id,
                    path('app_mail_label', { id: label.id, account: account.id, unread: 1 }),
                ) }}
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
</nav>
